  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 PM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: Showers and isolated thunderstorms continue early \nthis afternoon with considerable cloud debris across the CWA. Latest \nshort term guidance continues to struggle to clear out enough of the \nwarm sector as the prevalence of a persistent 40 knot LLJ through \nthe CWA may just be too strong to overcome.  This may result in a \nmuch lower surface based storm threat in the far southern CWA. There \nmay still be narrow window, but feel this may end up closer to I-80 \nthan Highway 20. Further west, a surface cold front continues it's \ntrek southeast.  Clearing ahead of this front in south central SD \nmight be just enough to spark a thunderstorm or two towards \nChamberlain and Gregory late this afternoon.  Any storm should \nweaken quickly as it moves east. \n\nTONIGHT:  While stronger surface based storms may just be south of \nthe CWA, we'll need to watch for a lower probability elevated storm \nrisk late in the evening and early in the overnight hours.  A \nsecondary wave moves into Nebraska after 04Z, with soundings showing \nat least the potential for a few stronger updrafts when lifted off \nthe 850:800 mb layer early in the overnight.  Given reasonable shear \nat that time, an isolated large hail threat could develop over the \nfar southeastern portions of the CWA. The cold front will push \nthrough the remainder of the CWA overnight, with a cooler northwest \nwind developing. \n\nTUESDAY: High pressure will be in control of the CWA on Tuesday, \nwith variable cloud cover leading to near normal highs around 70 \ndegrees.  \n\nWEDNESDAY: Mid-lvl flow turns slightly to the southwest on \nWednesday, allowing modest return flow to push low-lvl moisture back \nnorthward. While lift itself looks weak, a few showers and isolated \nstorms may develop through the day.  Soundings also show a high \nprobability for lower stratus which may limit the overall \ntemperature climb in the afternoon. \n\nTHURSDAY-FRIDAY: A similar setup continues for Thursday with broad \nbut weak warm advection persisting through the Tri-State area on \nThursday. Occasional light showers may develop north of a stalled \nwarm front over central and eastern Nebraska.  With low-lvl \ntemperatures climbing slightly, we may see a small uptick in high \ntemperatures towards the mid-upr 70s. The fly in the ointment \nhere is the persistent southeasterly surface wind and cloud \ncover, which could lower the high temperatures towards the \nlower to middle 70s instead. \n\nBy late Thursday night and Friday, a stronger wave ejects out of the \nCentral Rockies inducing stronger return flow and a sharpening of \nmoisture advection back into the Dakotas.  The nocturnal LLJ should \ninduce scattered to numerous non-severe showers and thunderstorms \ninto Friday morning.  With surface low pressure forming over the \nwestern Dakotas during the day, a warm front may surge northward \nacross the CWA by mid-day allowing temperatures to quickly climb \ninto the 80s.  This increased instability combined with a cold front \nthat begins to move southeast late in the afternoon and evening\ncould spark a few strong to severe storms into Friday evening. \nThough the quality of the warm sector, duration of morning \nstratus, always make severe weather risks this time of year more\ndifficult to pinpoint this far out. \n\nSATURDAY-SUNDAY: A stronger trough dips into the Dakotas for the \nweekend which favors lowering temperatures and more isolated to \nscattered shower development at times.  The severe weather risks \nwill remain low next weekend.",
